# Copy to .env.local and fill in real values. Never commit .env.local, .env, .env.production, or
# any file with real secrets in it — only this file (.env.example, placeholders only) is tracked.
#
# Every variable below is grouped by exposure level, not by feature:
#
#   PUBLIC       — NEXT_PUBLIC_* only. Next.js inlines these into the browser bundle at build
#                  time. Visible to anyone in devtools. Only ever put a value here that's safe for
#                  a stranger to read.
#   SERVER-ONLY  — read via `getServerEnv()` (src/lib/env.server.ts, which imports the
#                  `"server-only"` package — importing it from a Client Component fails the build,
#                  verified directly). Never inlined into the client bundle. Not necessarily a
#                  secret (e.g. EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS is server-only but shows up in every email you
#                  send), just never sent to the browser as app data.
#   SECRET       — server-only AND sensitive. Treat exactly like a password: only in
#                  `wrangler secret put` / the Cloudflare dashboard for production, never in a
#                  committed file, never logged, never returned in an API response.
#
# See CLOUDFLARE_DEPLOYMENT.md's "Environment variables" section for how these get into a
# deployed Cloudflare Worker (short version: NEXT_PUBLIC_* need to be present wherever `next
# build` runs; everything else is a `wrangler secret put <NAME>` on Cloudflare, independent of
# whatever's in your local .env.local).

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The app throws immediately at startup (getServerEnv()'s Zod validation) if any of these are
# missing — there is no fallback/default for any variable in this section.

# Supabase — https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/_/settings/api
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=                  # PUBLIC — your project's URL, not sensitive on its own.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=             # PUBLIC — designed to be public; every RLS policy in
                                            # this app's migrations is what actually protects data,
                                            # not secrecy of this key.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=                 # SECRET — bypasses Row Level Security entirely. Only
                                            # ever read by src/lib/supabase/admin.ts
                                            # (createAdminClient), which is server-only and never
                                            # imported by anything Client-Component-reachable. If
                                            # this leaks, rotate it in the Supabase dashboard
                                            # immediately — it's a full database bypass, not just
                                            # an API key.

# Site
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000 # PUBLIC — this app's own canonical URL. Also read
                                            # server-side (forgotPasswordAction builds the
                                            # password-reset link from it) — getting this wrong in
                                            # production doesn't just mis-render something client-
                                            # side, it emails customers a broken reset link. Set to
                                            # the real production domain (e.g.
                                            # https://digitalsubsbd.com) before building for
                                            # production — see CLOUDFLARE_DEPLOYMENT.md.

# ── Optional — safe defaults, only set to change behavior ─────────────────────────────────────

# Transactional email — defaults to logging to the console (no credentials needed) if unset, so
# local development and a first deploy both work with zero email configuration.
EMAIL_PROVIDER=console                     # SERVER-ONLY, not secret. "console" or "resend".
EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=no-reply@digitalsubsbd.com  # SERVER-ONLY, not secret — appears in every sent email.
EMAIL_FROM_NAME="Digital Subs BD"          # SERVER-ONLY, not secret — appears in every sent email.
# https://resend.com/api-keys — only required when EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend (validated in
# services/email/provider.ts, not by the schema, so a console-provider deployment never needs it).
RESEND_API_KEY=                            # SECRET.

# ── Not environment variables in this app — see CLOUDFLARE_DEPLOYMENT.md before adding any ────
#
# Payment configuration: checkout is manual bKash/Nagad/Rocket "Send Money" + a screenshot upload
# reviewed by staff, not a payment gateway API integration — there is no code anywhere that reads
# a payment credential from process.env. The Send Money numbers shown at checkout are
# `siteConfig.payment` in src/constants/site.ts (placeholder numbers — replace with the real
# merchant/personal numbers there before launch, not here). If a real gateway (bKash/Nagad/
# SSLCommerz API) is ever integrated, its credentials belong in this file at that point, as
# SECRET server-only variables validated by src/lib/env.server.ts — don't add unused placeholder
# vars for a feature that doesn't exist yet; it's a false signal that something is configured when
# nothing reads it.
#
# WhatsApp configuration: the support number is DB-backed (Settings.whatsappNumber, editable at
# /admin/settings, read via getPublicSettings() in src/lib/settings.ts), not an environment
# variable — deliberately, so it can change without a redeploy. siteConfig.links.whatsapp in
# src/constants/site.ts is only a hardcoded fetch-failure fallback. Don't add a WHATSAPP_NUMBER
# env var; update the number from /admin/settings instead.
#
# Cloudflare configuration: this app's Cloudflare-specific settings (the R2 bucket name, the
# three Durable Object bindings, compatibility flags) live in wrangler.jsonc, not in an env file —
# they're Worker infrastructure, not application secrets. The runtime values for every SERVER-ONLY
# and SECRET variable above still need to be set on Cloudflare too, via `wrangler secret put
# <NAME>` — see CLOUDFLARE_DEPLOYMENT.md's "Environment variables" section for the full list of
# commands and the build-time-vs-runtime distinction.
